cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this work, please cite it as below."
type: software
title: "Bow Draw: where the carries of the central binomial coefficients catch up with the number of rows"
version: "1.0"
date-released: "2026-11-23"
doi: "10.5281/zenodo.24451375"
authors:
  - given-names: "James"
    family-names: "Augustus"
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0009-0002-7286-7730"
    email: "jamesaugustus1313@gmail.com"
repository-code: "https://github.com/JamesAugustus/bow-draw"
abstract: >-
  Fix a base b ≥ 2. For every row k of Pascal's triangle, add ⌊k/2⌋ and ⌈k/2⌉ in base b and count
  the carries; let E_b(n) be the total over the rows 1 to n. The difference E_b(n) − n first falls,
  reaches a minimum near b³ and then crosses zero at an exact point n(b), given by a formula that
  depends only on b modulo 6; for even b, n(b) = b³ + b² + b − 2. For a prime p, n(p) is the point
  from which p^n divides the product of the central binomial coefficients C(1, 0)·C(2, 1)···C(n, ⌊n/2⌋).
  For every odd base and for b = 2 the existence, the location and the uniqueness are proved by hand;
  for even b ≥ 4 the existence is a computer-assisted proof and the uniqueness is measured for b < 2000.
  The package contains the paper in English and Turkish, the companion text FactorX, all programs
  (Python, C, Vulkan) with their raw output, checks over the 51 Mersenne primes up to 2^136279841 − 1,
  and a teaching demo.
keywords:
  - number theory
  - Pascal's triangle
  - central binomial coefficients
  - carries
  - Kummer's theorem
  - p-adic valuation
  - digit sums
  - Mersenne primes
